This is a tribute to UTA and the memories and experiences I had here many years ago, which are important parts of my life that I cherish a lot. It is loosely derived from my UTAC series, which I made back then (read about it here), but you do not need to know about it to read this.
Extensive author's notes will be at the bottom, where I will tell my story and talk a bit about this piece and why I wrote it.
In the meantime, I hope you enjoy.
————————

“The inbound train is now arriving at: Platform A. Please step back from the yellow line.”

A monotonous voice echoed as it broke the long silence that had settled in the station. As the sun emerged from the horizon, gentle rays of sunlight cast themselves onto the platform’s gray and yellow marble tiles. A playful breeze flowed through the station as it carried a wad of paper from one side of the floor to the other. Suddenly, the breeze was replaced with a rush of winds brought about by the arrival of a train. Sharp, mechanical screeching rang out on the tracks, growing louder and louder until the train came to a standstill. Its many doors opened simultaneously after a short jingle.

A lone figure emerged from the train. The man walked out onto the platform, holding a large brown notebook against his chest. While his eyes quickly scanned the deserted surroundings, he instinctively walked towards one of the exits, with no hint of the station being foreign to him. The exit led out onto the street, still devoid of other persons. As the sun rose behind him, the clouds cast over the sky began to slowly lift, revealing a tower in the distance, several blocks away in the city.

The figure trotted through the city’s crisscross of intersections and alleyways. The traffic lights alternated red and green as they faithfully followed their programming. The man paid them no mind as he kept on walking across the empty street. His eyes scanned the signs on the buildings and roads around him, their names long having faded beyond recognition. His mind tried to fill in the names from jumbled words scattered across different memories, to no avail.

Eventually, the man reached a series of nondistinct apartment blocks, all of which were weathered and decaying, as if no one had lived in them for a long time. Some were completely boarded up, obscuring any signs of life that their previous inhabitants may have left. He briefly paused as he looked up at one of the windows, seemingly trying to make out something behind the wooden planks, before continuing down the street. Finally, he stopped before the entrance staircase to one of the blocks. Walking up the steps revealed a hallway lined with wooden doors in varying stages of rot and wear. Except for the sunlight at the entrance, the hall was unlit. As he stopped before one of the doors, he drew a key from his pocket to open it. As he entered and closed the door behind him, the gentle, cool morning scent outside was replaced with the smell of old wood and paper. The room was a small one, furnished with only a bed, desk, and a few drawers, all of which were discolored and worn. Bits of dust shimmered in the small rays of sunlight peeking out from behind the window. The man inhaled; the room had an earthy aroma, strong but not overpoweringly so. He then sat down on a stool and placed his notebook on the desk.

The notebook was bound in a worn leather cover. Plastic bookmark notes of many colors jutted out from the edges of the different pages in varying positions. The book was much lighter than its massive size would suggest. The reason for such became clear as the man flipped back and forth between the different bookmarks. Some contained indistinct titles and dividers that preceded absent pages ripped from the book, leaving behind tear marks. Some had pages with squares drawn using black marker, each in just the right size to fit a polaroid photo. As the book laid open on the table, the man rifled through the various drawers in the room. The process disturbed the thin layer of dust that had seeped inside them and settled onto their contents: manuscripts, notes, drafts, pages, photos… All of them were stained with a discolored yellow and felt wrinkled and rough to the touch, yet fit perfectly into the notebook once the right places for them were found.

The man continued for some time. At the end of the process, the notebook had become noticeably heavier. Polaroid photos captioned with black ink were stuck onto the various pages with thin pieces of duct tape, filling up all the squares. Yet some of the written pages were still missing from the various sections. As he closed the book and picked it back up, the man adjusted himself and shook off his fatigue as he motioned towards the door. Just before leaving, he lingered to take in the scenery of the room. In his mind, he ran through a checklist of all the places he needed to check for belongings, and instinctively wondered if he had forgotten any. He also tried to snapshot the moment - the earthy smell of old wood and paper, the rays of sunlight escaping from behind the window and dancing on the bed. Finally, he shut the door gently yet firmly, its lock clicking back into place.

Outside, the gentle dawn sunlight had been replaced with the bursting radiance of noon. A vibrant blue extended across the cloudless skies. The tower in the distance could now be seen clearly, standing above the buildings surrounding it. The man began walking with purpose in the tower’s direction. As he did, he approached a large scramble crossing flanked by a series of buildings, and he stood at its center to observe. A handful of figures moved back and forth along the white lines drawn onto the asphalt - the first signs of life the man had seen since arriving at the city. The surrounding buildings were empty and showed signs of prolonged disuse, yet the billboards and signs above them still played vibrant, colorful footage on loop. Indistinct robotic voices played from their speakers, their words blending together into white noise - announcements, promotions, messages, advertisements… The man drew a polaroid from one of the marked pages and oriented himself to match the perspective in the photo. It depicted the same crossing, but now filled with crowds of indistinct people. While they were barely legible, the names on the surrounding buildings and businesses still glowed in varying colors of neon.

The man was lost in thought as he seemed to imagine himself in the square at that moment in time, standing in the midst of the hustle and bustle of people going about their days. Finally, he lowered the photo to see the crossing in its present state. As he did, he caught sight of a peculiar figure in the distance: a woman in a blue and brown dress adorned with yellow stars. Her right hand held a purple artbook with a pen clipped onto its cover. A passing breeze lightly disturbed her short amethyst-colored hair and the small hat that rested upon it. The noon sunlight reflected off of her monocle as she approached. While the woman’s appearance was foreign to him, her presence and demeanor felt distantly familiar. He seemed to have caught her attention as well.

The man put the polaroid back into his book to greet the approaching figure.

“Excuse me, miss, but do we happen to know each other?”
The woman stopped before him, her face wearing a gentle, mysterious smile.

“Good afternoon, sir. How peculiar - I was just about to ask you likewise.”

The man smiled back, his voice a mix of relief and contemplation. “Oh, I see… Though, all I remember is that we may have met a long time ago.”

“All is well. I can recall a similar memory, after which I have been away from here for many years.”

The woman began to open her artbook and unclip the pen attached to it.

“May I request something of you? I wish to discover more about the lives of those who have dwelled here. Much has transpired after my departure, yet few people remain to recount their stories. Would it be too much trouble for me to accompany you?”

“Sure, I don’t mind. I just have some business here to take care of today. Right now, I am headed there.”

The man pointed towards the tower peeking out from behind the buildings flanking the square. Taking one hand off his heavy notebook caused his other hand to lose balance, and he tipped the book downwards. As he re-secured the book, a badge fell out from one of the pages.

“Oh, this thing… I should return it. I guess a detour won’t hurt.” He remarked with a hint of surprise.

The woman motioned over to pick up the badge from the ground. As it sat in the palm of her hand, sunlight glistened off of its laminated surface.

“May I have this for a moment while we proceed? Its design is… intriguing.” The man simply nodded as he headed down one of the crossing’s paths. The woman followed behind him as the badge rested against the left page of her open artbook. On the right, the tip of her pen rested on the paper as she began to draw. As the two departed the crossing, they glanced up to find that the billboards were now displaying a monotonous blue, the noise from their speakers having long since ceased. The square was now filled with only a dull silence, interrupted by the occasional lone passerby.

The streets outside the crossing were once again deserted. Nondistinct buildings and shops stood on both sides, their desolate exteriors obscuring any identifiable features. The woman was instead focused on the badge that she was drawing. It had the shape of a shield and was coated with a laminated metal that glistened in the sunlight. The badge’s color was one of deep purple surrounded by white borders. In the middle was some kind of design - a winged circle floating above three small triangles.
The woman lifted her pen from the page as she completed the sketch - a shield with the design in the middle. When she looked up, she saw that they had arrived at a building with the same emblem painted on its opaque glassy exterior. Some text was written above the emblem, though it has faded beyond recognition. She returned the badge to the man in front of her, who approached the building’s entrance. A pair of automated glass doors, despite their weathered appearance, threw themselves open as the two entered.

The building’s interior was briefly shrouded in darkness before lights on the ceiling stirred back to life upon sensing the newcomers. A monotonous office-space white permeated the room and its decor - walls, ceiling, lights... The short walk from the entrance to the receptionist’s desk passed rows of black waiting chairs in the middle of the room. A tall plant rested next to the reception desk, its leaves having withered into dry, brown husks. The room had a distinctly artificial smell, a mix of air-conditioning, leather and plastic. The air was thick, like it had been undisturbed for months, maybe even years.

“Welcome. Please take a number and be seated until your number is called.”

The man did not pay the automated voice speaking over the PA system any mind as he proceeded to the reception desk. It was lightly furnished - only a fixture where a computer monitor was likely supposed to rest, and a pair of ballpoint pens chained to a stand glued to the counter’s surface. Behind the counter was where the receptionist was supposed to be sitting. There rested an old mouse and keyboard, both neatly wrapped in their own wires and covered in a layer of dust. Next to them was a discolored white mug, its bottom caked brown with rings of dried coffee stains. An office chair sat empty, facing the guests. The man reached behind the counter and rested the badge next to the mug.

“Did you use to work here?”

“Yes. I hope they didn’t mind me keeping the badge for so long.”

“What were your responsibilities?”

“Oh… It’s been so long, I don’t really remember. All I can recall is that we used to help out around the city.”

Instead of turning to leave, the man motioned over to a series of portraits neatly framed on the walls, next to the long-empty water cooler. The frames were worn and discolored, obscuring the faces and inscriptions underneath, but one could make out that they were awards for distinguished men and women in uniform. The man looked at each photo for a long while.

“Your former colleagues?”

“Yes…” The man seemed to want to say more, but trailed off, as if preoccupied with something on his mind.

“Were you close with them?”

“Well…” He turned to his notebook and flipped through the bookmarks, before opening to a page with a series of polaroid photos.

“I wouldn’t really say that. But we did spend a lot of time together. I enjoyed their company - hopefully they felt the same way about me.”

The white edges of the photos were filled with a myriad of signatures. Some were made with thick black lines from a marker. Others were made with thin, graceful pen strokes of varying colors.

“From what I see… I believe they did.” The woman lifted a photo and briefly examined it before placing it back onto the page. The people in the photos were visibly smiling and content.

The man closed the book and looked back at the framed portraits on the walls. 

“I hope they are doing okay, wherever they are.” He exhaled deeply before motioning towards the entrance.

As they left the building and returned outside, they saw the sun beginning to hover over the top of the tower in the distance, casting long shadows onto the street. The man listlessly gazed towards it as he continued walking, his arms still wrapped around the book in his chest. Next to him, the woman flipped through her artbook, past pages of incomplete sketches, before stopping at a completed drawing. She inspected it briefly before closing the book and turning to the man.

“By any chance, are you visiting the memorial?” 

The question evoked an expression of curiosity from the man as he briefly stopped.

“Yes, actually…” Then one of solemnity. “I heard not a lot of people visit anymore.”

“Indeed... ” Her contemplative expression betrayed that she had more to say. “Let’s go then, shall we?”

Along the street leading to the tower laid a series of shops. Some had large glass windows painted with stylized names and logos, most of which had deteriorated into bits of paint and paper. Behind these windows often sat display cases and stands, now empty or knocked over. “Sorry! We are Closed” signs still hung crookedly over some of the shop doors. Others had dormant neon signs too dark to read pointing at them, beckoning the two passerbys.

As the pair approached and gazed upon the tower, the sun sank behind its peak. Before them was a set of wide, imposing steps leading up to the courtyard outside the tower’s entrance. 
The tower was a thin, spiraling building, its height forcing the two to fully look up into the sky. White office lights illuminated the various rooms along the tower’s body. A lone glass elevator ascended to one of the more well-lit floors, where figures could be seen moving about the windows. After admiring the tower, the two continued past the steps, stopping at a hedge just the street over.

Behind the hedge was a series of granite pillars carved into the earth against stone platforms. The grass around them was overgrown and mixed with varying weeds and wild flowers. In the distance, the sun had begun sinking into the horizon, its gentle orange glow illuminating the dozen or so names etched into the pillars. Under the stone platforms, hidden beneath the tall grass, one could make out dried and torn remnants of various offerings - incense, letters, notes, flowers… The only offerings on the stone platforms were carnation flowers, one for each pillar, still fresh and vibrantly purple. As the woman removed her hat, a fresh carnation petal fell out from inside it.

Stopping before the pillars, the man pulled out something from his coat pocket: origami flowers, folded with paper similar to that of his notebook. Lines of text written in black ink could be seen along the flowers’ sides. After the man placed a flower on each platform, the two stood before the pillars with solemn expressions, facing each other.

“I would like to ask you something. Who were these people to you?” Hearing the question, the man stood with his hand against his chin for a few moments with a contemplative expression.

“When I first got here, things were messy… I was still figuring myself out. What I wanted to do, whether I had made a mistake coming here. It’s a long story, but…” He held the notebook closer to his chest.

“They did a lot to help out people like me. So many people, all over the city. People that they had never met. People that didn’t always appreciate their help…” As he said it, his gaze unconsciously drifted downwards.

“It means a lot to me.” He adjusted himself upon finishing. The woman simply nodded in response, seemingly pondering what he said.

“What about you?” The man’s question seemed to interrupt her train of thought.

“They were many things to me.” After a pause, she slowly walked towards the pillars with a solemn expression.

“Leaders. Mentors. Mediators.” She crouched down to one of the carnation flowers and adjusted its petals. “Friends.” The man’s expression became one of intrigue as he slowly came up beside her. She turned to face him.

“The feelings you speak of. Aimlessness, uncertainty towards oneself, hurting and being hurt. They also shared these feelings, these experiences. They questioned whether they were enough. Whether they deserved their responsibility. Yet they persevered.” The woman’s gaze turned towards the distant horizon. The twilight sky was now a gradient of colors, split between the orange glow of the sun and the darkness of night.

“And like us, they moved onto greener pastures.” 

The woman looked down at the artbook in her hands, opened to a completed drawing. The paint on it was fresh, like it had dried only today. It depicted a group of people standing side-by-side in front of what seemed to be the tower, their arms around each other’s shoulders, their expressions bursting with cheer. A figure with the woman’s likeness stood to the side of the group, her arms folded in front of her.

The woman gazed at the drawing, a hint of longing in her eyes. Still crouching in front of the pillars, the woman removed the drawing from the book and rested it under one of the carnation flowers before standing up.

The man spoke as the two turned to leave.

“You know, even though I never got to meet them, I have always wondered. About what they have been doing after they left. What they’re doing now.”

As she put her hat back on, the woman looked up to the sky to see a blanket of darkness slowly overtaking the sun, whose glow had receded almost completely beyond the horizon.

“I have wondered as much myself. For a long time.” Her voice was tinged with melancholy.

A long quiet settled around them as they continued walking down the path before them, seemingly without purpose. As nightfall settled in completely, the street lamps around them lit up in unison.

“I have one more place to check out.” The man broke the silence as he pointed to their destination at the end of the path: a narrow multiuse building, standing slightly taller than the ones around it. 

To the side of the building’s entrance was an elevator. Next to the elevator was a board covered with various old papers and posters stuck atop one another. Upon pressing the elevator’s button, it dinged open to reveal a narrow metallic interior, into which the two stepped, and the man pressed the button for the topmost floor. As the doors closed, a dull mechanical hum echoed all around them. The noise persisted until the elevator came to a stop with another ding, its doors opening to reveal almost complete darkness.
“One second, I’ll get the lights.” The man stepped into the room and ran his hands along the walls before pausing at some kind of switch.

As the woman stepped out of the elevator, the darkness was replaced by the glow of warm fluorescent lights. Her eyes adjusted to see that she was facing a small bar with a wooden countertop. The man was running his finger along its surface, after which he rested his notebook on it.

“Make yourself at home. Do you want something to drink?” The man said as he moved behind the counter.

“A spot of tea would be delightful. Spearmint, please.” The woman seated herself on one of the bar stools and began taking in her surroundings as the man rummaged through the bar’s cabinets. A row of wine glasses hung over the bar, their surfaces dry and weathered. The glasses swayed gently under the evening breeze from the open balcony behind her. Bookshelves as tall as the ceiling covered the room’s walls. The shelves were filled to the brim with documents, files and books of varying sizes and colors, their spines discolored from lack of use. What seemed to be a fireplace was also dug into the wall, though now filled with only charred debris fragments. Wooden tables and chairs were neatly arranged around the room, not dissimilar to those found in a cafe. The room overall was small, with only a few foot paces needed to go from one side of the room to the other.

“Here you go.” The man reached over from behind the counter and placed down a drink for the woman: a cup of tea, with a spearmint leaf garnish on the side of the glass and an unwrapped cube of sugar resting on the saucer. Next to hers was another glass filled with dark coffee, heaped full with ice cubes. The man moved to his chair, but did not sit down, instead opening his book to one of the pages.

“Would you mind waiting for a bit? I need to find something. In the meantime, you can take a look at my notebook if you want.”

“I do not mind.”

The man stepped towards the bookcases and began moving his fingers along the files and books.

As the woman lifted her cup, hot steam accompanied the fresh scent of spearmint emanating from the cup. The tea had a refreshing yet invigorating taste that warmed the soul. 

After placing the cup back down, the woman looked at the open book on the counter. The polaroid photos on the pages showed the interior of this very room at various points in time, all taken from the same angle. In most of them, people were seated at the tables with their drinks, enjoying a book or writing their own. A figure bearing the man’s likeness could be invariably seen in some of the photos, sometimes writing on one of the tables, sometimes standing behind the bar.

One particular photo depicted the man in the room, sitting next to a taller masculine figure dressed in a brown tailored suit. The two were looking out the balcony window, its surface opaque and obscured by a layer of ice and snow. The lights were off, leaving the room to be illuminated by only the roaring fireplace. A notebook resembling the man’s current one rested on one of the tables.

Turning to the next page revealed the beginning of a series of written works. The handwriting was rough and barely legible, but seemed to become more refined as the pages went on. The pen and ink used also changed: ballpoint, fountain, blue, black… The pages themselves were in varying conditions. Some were off-color but remained smooth to the touch. Some showed signs of wear from prolonged exposure to the elements. Some were mangled and wrinkled, like they had been crumpled up and straightened back out. A chunk of pages was missing entirely, indicated by the tear marks left behind.

The woman stopped at one of the pages and began to read, occasionally stopping to take a sip of tea. Behind her, the man was taking some pages out of the shelves and sorting them into a pile on the ground. When the woman was finished with her tea, the man walked over with the pages in his hand.

“Sorry to keep you waiting.”

“All is well, sir. The tea was excellent.”

The man stowed the pages inside the notebook before closing it. The pages seemed to fit the missing chunk.

“Care to join me outside?” The man said as his hand flicked a switch on the wall. The balcony was gently illuminated by string lights, hung over a wooden counter fixed against the glass railing. The woman nodded in response as the two stepped out into the cool night air.

They peeked beyond the railing to catch a view of the city, stretching far beyond the horizon. Lights ran along the body of glass skyscrapers standing in the distance. Roads and highways were illuminated by uniform rows of street lamps. Buildings of varying shapes and sizes filled the gaps between well-lit landmarks. Many were completely unlit, except for the automated red warning lights dimly blinking at their peaks. The night was still; no one seemed to be moving about. The two sat for some time to enjoy the silence.

“This view reminds me of when I was in the tower.” The woman was resting her chin against the railing, her gaze directed at the tower standing above the buildings around it. “You really could see the whole city from up there.”

“It seems like a really cool place. I always wondered what it was like inside.” The man had just finished a sip of coffee.

“Quite different from here. This place is… more cozy.” The woman said with a smile.

“I’m glad you like it. I used to come here a lot back then.” The man smiled back with a hint of awkwardness.

“What did you do here?” The man pondered for some time for an answer.

“Just reading, at first. Then I decided I wanted to write for myself too. Made some friends here along the way.” As he recounted, he idly swirled a teaspoon in his glass as he gazed out into nowhere in particular, seemingly in reminiscence.

“I kept writing for a bit. Until I left.”

“Why did you stop?”

“Life.” The man answered with a deadpan voice, though followed by a chuckle. The response also elicited a small laugh from the woman.

“I don’t know for sure, really.” The man’s expression returned to a contemplative one. A hint of melancholy escaped the man as he exhaled. His lips shook for a bit, as if he was trying to find the courage to say something. “Maybe it’s just a part of my life that’s behind me now.” He continued staring out into the night sky.

“It’s a shame. I think your stories are quite enjoyable.”

“Oh, thank you. No one’s read them in a really long time.” A slight bashfulness could be seen in the man’s expression.

“For my part…” The woman motioned to her own book at her side. She turned it to one of the first pages, which were covered with crude doodles of various subjects. 

“I have a similar story. Many years ago, I first explored art as a hobbyist here.” She continued as she slowly flipped through the book. She seemed to have much to say, but was letting the pages speak for themselves. 

“The journey was a long one. The people here…” She settled into a cadence as she flipped through the dozens upon dozens of pages. The sketches increased in complexity and detail as she went on. Over time, more colors were added, and a distinct artstyle began to emerge. “Some encouraged me. Some inspired me. For that, I am grateful.”

Finally, she stopped at a page with an unfinished drawing. Its paint had barely begun to dry on the page. “Looking back now, I can’t say with certainty that I am proud of my past work. But it did set me on a path that I am still on.”

The man leaned over for a closer look at the drawing. “We all have to start somewhere. For what it’s worth, I think you’re a good artist.” 
“My thanks to you.” Her response was courteous, with a hint of uncertainty.

“You know… What you just said. I haven’t thought of it that way before.” The man spoke, but did not directly face the woman, instead casting his gaze upon the night sky.

“How your time here made you who you are right now. I guess the same should go for me. Even if I don’t write anymore. I just thought that all of this was in the past. That I needed to move on with my life and leave it behind.”

The man turned to the notebook on the counter, his fingers gently running along the surface of a page. “I even left everything I wrote back here.” He lifted his glass of coffee and took a sip as he finished.

The woman glanced at the notebook again. It had become noticeably thicker after all its pages were recovered. “Yet you returned for them today.”

“Yeah. I heard they’re stopping the train lines here soon.” The man’s gaze fell back down to the city under them, sprawling yet devoid of activity, as if frozen in time. He wore a pained and melancholic expression as he spoke. “When I got the news, I felt it. Like this place won’t be around for long. Like everything here will just… go away.”

A long exhale escaped the man’s lips and disturbed the cool night air. He finally turned to the woman. “You feel it too, don’t you? That’s why you wanted to find out more about the people here. There aren’t many left anymore.” She simply solemnly nodded in response.

“It has been an inescapable feeling for me since I returned here. I can see it everywhere I go.” The woman sighed with a tired expression. As she stared blankly at the city, for just a brief moment, she seemingly became acutely aware of how barren and lifeless the scenery before her eyes was, before regaining her composure. “The best we can do is preserve the memory of this place. Hence my undertaking.”

“Yeah. It’s also why I came back for this. Even though I haven’t thought about it in years before now.” His hand was brushing against the rough edges of the notebook’s pages.

“Maybe not much will come of it. Maybe I’ll just stuff it away and forget about it again. But it still feels wrong, you know? To let it all just disappear.” The woman firmly nodded in response.

A silence, though a comfortable one, eventually settled between the two, as if a weight had been lifted from their shoulders. A sense of relief slowly washed over them as they looked up into the sky. The dark blue expanse was intermittently interrupted by the faint glow of a star. Sometimes, they focused their vision in their attempts to make out more, hidden in the darkness. 

The woman, seemingly suddenly struck with a sense of recollection, opened and flipped through her artbook again in search of something, before settling on a page.

“By any chance, do you know of this place?” She showed the drawing to the man. It depicted a sun-kissed beach covered in smooth white sand. A lone set of footprints across the sand led to an indistinct figure standing in the distance next to the water, its surface calm and awash with a gentle blue. The water extended beyond the horizon, further than the eye could see. The figure seemed to be staring out towards the ocean, their expression contemplative.

The man simply shook his head in response. “Still, it’s beautiful, is it not?” The woman began speaking with a fondness in her voice as she continued. “It was quite out of the way. I seemed to be the only soul that was ever present there.”

“When… things became too much to bear…” A pained expression could be briefly seen on her face before she continued. “...I often visited.” She brought the drawing closer to her.

“When you are there, it feels like… Everything else melts away. The soft feeling of sand pushing up against the soles of your feet. The way the wind caresses your face. The refreshing scent of the ocean filling your senses. The gentle sound of the waves washing against the shore... It’s only you, alone, in this corner of the world.”

The man inspected the drawing more thoroughly, seemingly attempting to conjure the described feelings in his mind. “I can see what you mean. Imagining myself there… I think I would have really enjoyed it too.”

“This place went away a long time ago.” The woman’s voice became more solemn as she rested the artbook back onto the counter. “I doubt anyone else knows of it.” She caressed the paper to straighten some wrinkles on it before closing the book.

“I get what you’re saying. I mean, even this view here, right now…” The man said as he put up a polaroid photo before his left eye, seemingly comparing it to the view before him, before handing the photo to the woman.

“It was really different back then. I used to come out here to unwind. Usually late at night when everyone else is gone.” It showed a night view from the same balcony they were on, but of a much more vibrant version of the city. All manners of lights and signs dotted crowded streets below. Through lit windows of offices and apartments, one could make out the people inside them going about their evening.

The woman inspected the photo as the man reminisced. “I came up here once after a really rough day. And it just… made me feel some kind of way.” His voice shook a little as he briefly paused. “Like… This giant city around me. Everything felt so alive. Lights, traffic, buildings… everywhere.” He seemed to struggle to find the right words to express himself. “When I saw it, it was like… All the things I was moody about. Work, school, long hours, deadlines, traffic… They felt so small in comparison.” Feelings of contentment and nostalgia washed over the man as he finished his sentence. “It’s not the same anymore, but… I still can’t forget that feeling.”

The woman spoke as she brought the photo closer. “When I see that view, I get a sense of the feeling you are describing. The feeling of… becoming acutely aware that your life is but a small part of this… vast, vibrant metropolitan expanse.” Her voice became a contemplative one.

“Take this office building for instance. At first glance, it is a mere collection of lights among many others.” She pointed to the windows on one of the buildings in the photo. They were close enough that one could make out the various shapes and colors inside. “But if you focus closely, you can glimpse the furniture in each room, each floor. Each a different intersection of various lives. When I see it, I wonder about what the people within are doing, what brought them there. If they cast their gaze outside the window, perhaps they would be able to see me as well. Or, perhaps, I would just be another light, in a sea of lights…” She held the photo farther from her again, bringing the whole city back into view, before handing the photo back to the man.

The man slowly nodded as he lowered the photo, his gaze still focused on it, as if he were trying to transport himself back to that space and time. Finally, once he returned the photo to the notebook, he looked back at the city before him in its current state. The building in the photo still stood in the same place. The rooms inside the windows were identically barren and devoid of furniture, a dull white illuminating them all.

Both seemed to be lost in thought as they stared back out into nothingness. Fatigue began to encroach upon their expressions, their eyes briefly fluttering. After a silent yawn, the man searched for a landmark in the distance, its shape resembling a station.

“I need to catch the train soon.” He let out a bittersweet chuckle as he lifted his notebook off the counter. “I have work tomorrow…” 

“You should proceed, then. I will remain here for a little while longer.” The woman shifted to face the man. He turned to leave and adjusted himself, albeit deliberately slowly, as if something was on his mind. The woman accompanied him as he walked back into the room.

“Hey.” He said to the woman as they stood before the elevator. “Thanks for keeping me company today.” His voice betrayed a hint of awkwardness, one that he initially attempted to suppress in his throat before simply letting it escape. “It means a lot to me.” As the words left him, he smiled with a relieved expression.

“Thank you as well, sir.” The woman smiled back. For a brief moment, her brows furrowed, as if she had too much she wanted to say but could not put it all into words. “For sharing your stories with me. And listening to mine.”

The man nodded in response before he stepped into the elevator.

“Perhaps, we will meet again another time.” The woman said as she waved, her voice bittersweet.

Under his breath, the man uttered a quiet “yeah” that failed to escape his throat. “See you when I see you.” He waved back as the woman’s silhouette vanished behind the closing elevator doors.

The man travelled the deserted streets on his way to the station. The journey was a quiet one, accompanied by only the sounds of his own footsteps. His gaze drifted towards the ground as he idly navigated to his destination, his mind preoccupied.

Before long, he stood before the station entrance. As he ascended the stairs to reach his train, he stopped halfway upon catching sight of something on another platform. A lone figure stood next to a train with their back turned to him. The figure, seemingly an older gentleman, wore a brown tailored suit and polished shoes. He held a briefcase in his right hand as he stood before the yellow line on the ground, seemingly waiting to board. A mechanical jingle rang out as the train doors began to open.

The sight of the figure filled the man with a sense of familiarity. It almost instinctively caused him to run over and extend his greetings, the kind one would share with an old friend. Yet the feeling was short-lived, swiftly replaced by insurmountable reluctance and uncertainty. He felt his legs unwilling to move, weighed down by some invisible anchor. He thought of calling out; a name attempted to escape his lips, but only whispers came out. After the brief struggle, the man resigned himself to being a mere bystander. The figure, who had begun to board, eventually disappeared out of sight after moving deeper into the train. After some time, a robotic voice rang out.

“The outbound train is now departing at: Platform B. Please step back from the yellow line.”

The man looked on as the doors closed. The train then began to stir to movement, rapidly picking up speed before becoming a blur of metal and colors. It eventually left the station, bound towards some destination unknown to the man. He exhaled as he continued to ascend the stairs, until another platform came into view.

A train with its doors open rested on the platform. The man made his way inside and seated himself facing the station interior. No one else was on the train.

As he placed down his notebook next to him and settled himself in his seat, he looked back towards the station’s interior. Its stillness was periodically interrupted by the movement of text displayed on the electronic boards overhead. He looked at the signs pointing to the exits and, one by one, attempted to recall memories of travelling through each, mentally tracing his steps through the station’s long-gone hustle-and-bustle.

“The outbound train is now departing at: Platform A. Please step back from the yellow line.”

His thoughts were interrupted by a voice echoing through the station, immediately followed by a jingle coming from the train’s speakers. The train doors began to close, shutting completely just as the jingle finished. Sounds of hydraulics and metal rang out as the train inched forward and began to accelerate. As the train approached full speed, the man could see the station’s interior becoming a blur of lights and colors speeding past the windows, before disappearing completely. A dull humming permeated the train’s interior.

The man turned around and looked through the window behind him. Before his eyes was an endless black only interrupted by the glow of a full moon. As the train’s interior lights dimmed, the man could begin to make out the glistening reflections of moonlight dancing across the surface of a watery expanse. The moon’s reflection was occasionally disturbed by waves gently shimmering across the ocean. As the train continued, the scenery slowly shifted from one side of the window to the other before finally vanishing, leaving nothing but pitch black in its wake.

The man settled back into his seat and opened the notebook on his lap. As his eyes glazed across the words and pictures on its pages, his eyelids began to weigh as fatigue settled in before long. His mind became a blur of thoughts and recollections. Images, sounds and sights swirled and mixed together as his consciousness slowly faded.

