Author's Notes
If you have finished the piece, thank you for reading. I have a lot more to say; about UTA, about this writing...
My Story
To start off, I spent a lot of time on UTA around 2017 - 2018. It was also when I first did creative writing as a hobby, starting with Undertale fanfictions.
Soon after, I met a lot of other people here. I entered groups and friendships. Eventually, I created UTAC to branch out from Undertale and experiment with writing about different things. It was also a way for me to engage with the people I knew, by including them as characters in stories that I wrote.
The main reason why UTAC existed and went on for as long as it did was because I cared a lot about the friends I had made and the communities that I had become a part of in UTA.
Eventually, though, I stopped doing creative writing entirely. Soon after, for various reasons, I left UTA as well. Since then, I have periodically come back to reminisce and leave my thoughts, which you can find on my wall.
Overall, despite the ups and downs, my time in UTA has always meant a lot to me. The time I spent, the stories I wrote, the people I met, the memories I made... All of it is an important part of my life. UTA helped me explore and find myself during a time in which I really, really needed it.
Fast forward to a late night in November 2025. I still hadn't properly gone back into creative writing as a hobby for ~7 years by that point. For some reason, I suddenly had the idea to go back and read some of my old UTA works. Which I did, and ended up actually enjoying. I had forgotten the contents of my stories almost entirely, so it was like reading them for the first time.
Then I realized the dire state that UTA (and Amino as a whole) was in. I was already aware of UTA's declining active user numbers and the Amino app's enshittification (what with all the ads) for some time, but what I saw in November felt very different. Post contents refused to load properly on my phone. When I open posts on my computer, they would often fail due to issues with the servers, which only got worse over time. Chatroom backgrounds no longer loaded. UTA active user numbers frequently stayed at 0 for prolonged periods. Featured post like counts hovered at less than 100 (for context, in 2017, they could get up to, on average, ~2000). People I knew who remained posted about the app being on its deathbed.
As someone who knows a bit about how apps and services work behind the scenes, it really worried me. The fact that basic, fundamental features of the app (e.g., VIEWING POSTS) did not work properly, to me, was a major red flag that Amino as a service had been effectively abandoned and left to die. It felt like the app was falling apart on a fundamental, technical level, and that it wouldn't ever get the support it needed to recover.
What would happen, then, to everything I left here? When I realized how screwed Amino seemed to be, I began backing things up. My works, the works of my friends, my bio, wall messages... I also left goodbyes to people that I used to know and care about, in some distant hope that they would somehow, some day, see it. Better that than to have Amino one day permanently shut down and then end up regretting not doing it.
This process and the feelings I had while going through it was what directly inspired me to write Hiraeth. I also wanted to have some closure for myself for the feelings I had towards UTA. Hearing me describe it all now, you may be able to see some connections with what I wrote. And now you know.
To those of you reading this, I hope I have been able to convey to you how much UTA means to me. If you are also an UTA vet and also feel strongly about it, I hope I have been able to capture and honor those feelings in some way.
UTAC References
TL;DR for UTAC: It is an "UTA but represented as a real city" concept.
- The city in Hiraeth represents, of course, UTA itself.
- The man represents, of course, me. The photos represent my memories, and the writings and notes are a stand-in for my UTA writings. The man returning to the city to collect things for his notebook are a parallel to my own process of backing things up from UTA.
- The woman is a representation of a character made by Gem, whom I met in UTA back then. We eventually found each other again outside Amino many years later, and they now go by a different name.
- The man in the brown suit is a representation of ATG, one of the people I engaged a lot with during my UTA days. He made this post not long before I started backing up my works, which contributed to my decision to do exactly that.
- The scramble square with the billboards is a nod to the Featured tab and the Curator's Review. (In UTAC, these things were represented as a variety of things: A display shop named "Curator's Review", billboards, loudspeakers...) Visually, it is heavily inspired by Shibuya Crossing in Tokyo, Japan.
- The badge and the man's workplace represent my time as a member of RGU.
- The tower is a nod to Soul Squad Tower, a recurring location in UTAC. It is where current Soul Squad members live and work.
- The memorial is a nod to the Soul Squad memorial, a recurring location in UTAC. It is where people go to pay respects to retired and departed Soul Squad members. In some entries, it is depicted as a cemetery.
- The cafe is a nod to the TAA book cafe, a recurring location in UTAC. The book cafe is a chill hangout spot for TAA members to come read and write.
- The image of the man in the cafe with snow and ice outside the balcony is a reference to UTAC Entry 7: Winter, which depicts a scenario of me wandering through a heavy snowing UTA city. I visit the TAA in that story.
